(Executive Summary) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
13 TL
(This project builds a system that can recognize a person's face to let them log in or) Tj
T*
(prove their identity -- the same idea behind Face ID on a phone or a fingerprint) Tj
T*
(scanner at an office door, but built entirely from free, open tools running on a) Tj
T*
(normal laptop, with no data ever leaving the machine.) Tj
T*
T*
(The problem it solves: many face-recognition systems can be tricked by simply) Tj
T*
(holding up a photo or playing a video of the real person. This project adds two) Tj
T*
(extra checks beyond just matching a face: a check that the photo itself is clear and) Tj
T*
(usable, and a check that a real, live person is actually in front of the camera right) Tj
T*
(now, not a picture or a recording.) Tj
T*
T*
(Does it work: yes, for the core flow. A person can register their face once, and) Tj
T*
(later have it recognized reliably, with the system correctly telling the right person) Tj
T*
(apart from someone else in internal testing. Real weaknesses were also found) Tj
T*
(through honest testing rather than assumed away -- most notably, one of the three) Tj
T*
("is this a real person" checks can be fooled by a specific kind of screen replay,) Tj
T*
(and fairness across different groups of people has not yet been fully addressed.) Tj
T*
(Those are stated openly in this report, not hidden.) Tj
T*
T*
(Honest current state: the underlying engineering is solid and has been tested with) Tj
T*
(real data throughout, real problems were found and fixed rather than assumed) Tj
T*
(away, and every known weakness is written down in this report. What remains is a) Tj
T*
(short, known list, not a mystery: a recorded demonstration video, and one final) Tj
T*
(confirmation pass on the most recent fixes.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 15 Tf
19 TL
(The Problem) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
13 TL
(Face verification means using a photograph of someone's face to prove they are) Tj
T*
(who they say they are, similar to how a password proves it on a website, except) Tj
T*
(the "password" is your face.) Tj
T*
T*
(The reason this is harder than it sounds is that a face-matching system alone can) Tj
T*
(be tricked. If the system's only job is checking whether a face matches the one on) Tj
T*
(file, someone could hold up a printed photo of the real person, play a video of) Tj
T*
(them, or show a photo on a phone screen, and the system might accept it, because) Tj
T*
(a photo of a face still looks like a face.) Tj
T*
T*
(This is why the project needed a second capability on top of matching, called) Tj
T*
(liveness detection: a way to check that a real, living person is actually in front of) Tj
T*
(the camera right now, not a photo, a video, or a screen. It also needed a third) Tj
T*
(capability, called image quality assessment, to make sure the photo being) Tj
T*
(checked is clear and usable in the first place, since a blurry or badly lit photo) Tj
T*
(can cause the wrong decision either way.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
