/F2 12 Tf
72 740 Td
16 TL
(Phase 2: Building the Basic Photo-Quality Checks) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
13 TL
(What we were trying to do: teach the system to reject a bad photo before doing) Tj
T*
(anything else with it -- too dark, too blurry, face turned too far sideways, more) Tj
T*
(than one face in frame, or part of the face covered.) Tj
T*
T*
(What we actually built: six separate checks, each looking at one specific thing:) Tj
T*
(overall brightness, sharpness \(called "blur" -- how out-of-focus the image is\),) Tj
T*
(head angle \(how far the face is turned or tilted, called "pose"\), whether the) Tj
T*
(face is centered and takes up enough of the frame, whether exactly one face is) Tj
T*
(visible, and whether part of the face looks covered or hidden.) Tj
T*
T*
(We did not guess the cutoff numbers for any of these checks. Real photos were) Tj
T*
(taken of a real person -- some good, some deliberately bad -- and the actual) Tj
T*
(numbers each type of photo produced were measured, with each threshold placed) Tj
T*
(in the real gap between "clearly good" and "clearly bad" photos.) Tj
T*
T*
(The real bug we found: the head-angle check uses a technique called solvePnP,) Tj
T*
(which works out the angle of a face in 3D space by matching known points on a) Tj
T*
(face -- like the nose tip and eye corners -- against a generic 3D model of a) Tj
T*
(face. When this was first tested against real video of a real face turning left) Tj
T*
(and right, the system reported faces as upside-down that were clearly) Tj
T*
(right-side-up on screen.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
