BT
/F2 12 Tf
72 740 Td
16 TL
(Phase 4: Face Matching and Registration) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
13 TL
(What we were trying to do: build the actual comparison that answers "does this) Tj
T*
(face belong to the person who signed up," plus the process for signing someone) Tj
T*
(up for the first time, called registration.) Tj
T*
T*
(What we actually built: each face is converted into a list of numbers, called an) Tj
T*
(embedding, using a well-established, pretrained model called ArcFace \(accessed) Tj
T*
(through a library called DeepFace\). Two embeddings from the same person's) Tj
T*
(face end up numerically close together; two embeddings from different people) Tj
T*
(end up far apart. Comparing two embeddings with a similarity score is how) Tj
T*
(matching works underneath.) Tj
T*
T*
(The cutoff for "close enough to count as a match" is not guessed. It was) Tj
T*
(calibrated using a real research dataset of face-pairs from different real people,) Tj
T*
(plotting how the system performs at every possible cutoff and choosing a) Tj
T*
(specific point deliberately, rather than picking a number that felt right.) Tj
T*
T*
(Registration also includes a duplicate check: before storing a new person's face,) Tj
T*
(the new face is compared against every already-registered face, and) Tj
T*
(registration is refused if it looks like the same person is trying to register) Tj
T*
(twice under a different name. This catches a simple but real kind of fraud at) Tj
T*
(very low extra cost, since the same comparison logic already exists. Figure 3,) Tj
T*
(next page, shows the complete pipeline built across Phases 2 to 4.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
