(Phase 6: Security and Legal Readiness Review) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
13 TL
(What we were trying to do: before calling this ready for a real client, ask a) Tj
T*
(harder question than "does it work" -- is it actually safe and legal to use with) Tj
T*
(real people's faces.) Tj
T*
T*
(What was missing, in plain terms: face data was being stored without) Tj
T*
(encryption, meaning if the storage file were ever accessed by someone who) Tj
T*
(should not have it, the raw face data inside would be directly readable.) Tj
T*
T*
(There was no way to delete someone's data if they asked for it to be removed.) Tj
T*
(This matters legally, not just technically -- most privacy laws covering) Tj
T*
(biometric data \(data about a person's body, like a fingerprint or a face\) give) Tj
T*
(people a legal right to have their data deleted on request, sometimes called) Tj
T*
(the "right to be forgotten.") Tj
T*
T*
(The backend system had no login protection of its own, meaning anyone who) Tj
T*
(could reach it over the network could call it directly, with no check on who) Tj
T*
(was asking.) Tj
T*
T*
(What was added to fix each one: face data is now encrypted before it is ever) Tj
T*
(saved to disk, using a standard, well-established encryption method, so the) Tj
T*
(stored file is unreadable without the correct key.) Tj
T*
T*
(Registration now requires an explicit, recorded consent step -- a checkbox the) Tj
T*
(user must tick -- and is refused outright if consent was not given. A real) Tj
T*
(delete function was added with two levels: a soft delete, which removes) Tj
T*
(someone from all future matching immediately but keeps a recoverable record,) Tj
T*
(and a hard delete, which permanently and irreversibly removes their data.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
